WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
The first film followed one gene.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.920
What changes when two genes travel through the same cross?

3
00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.480
The grid must track both allele pairs together.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.560
Mendel followed seed shape and seed color.

5
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.380
We will use capital R and small r for shape.

6
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.860
Capital Y and small y track color.

7
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:26.653
Each parent is heterozygous for both genes.

8
00:00:27.993 --> 00:00:31.593
Every sex cell receives one shape allele and one color allele.

9
00:00:32.717 --> 00:00:35.697
In this model, the genes assort independently.

10
00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:41.257
Receiving either shape allele does not affect which color allele joins it.

11
00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:43.697
That creates every pairing.

12
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:48.577
Predict how many different sex-cell types this parent can make.

13
00:00:49.777 --> 00:00:52.797
Follow every path from a shape allele to a color allele.

14
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:06.310
The paths make capital R capital Y, capital R small y, small r capital Y, and ry.

15
00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.350
Each type is equally likely here.

16
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.060
Each sex cell carries one allele for each gene.

17
00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.640
No path carries both shape alleles or both color alleles from one parent.

18
00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:22.413
Place those sex-cell types along both sides.

19
00:01:23.653 --> 00:01:26.233
The square expands to show every possible meeting.

20
00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:29.813
Each box receives one type from each parent.

21
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:32.687
Read each box gene by gene.

22
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:36.847
Combine the shape alleles first, then the color alleles.

23
00:01:37.927 --> 00:01:40.767
The finished genotype controls both visible traits.

24
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.840
Now group boxes by phenotype.

25
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.280
A dominant shape allele makes round seeds.

26
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.800
A dominant color allele makes yellow seeds in this model.

27
00:01:52.883 --> 00:01:55.123
The largest group is round and yellow.

28
00:01:56.403 --> 00:01:59.223
Two smaller groups carry one recessive phenotype.

29
00:02:00.523 --> 00:02:02.543
The smallest group is wrinkled and green.

30
00:02:03.633 --> 00:02:07.273
The smallest group needs recessive alleles at both genes.

31
00:02:08.573 --> 00:02:12.993
That combination occupies only the bottom corner of the organized grid.

32
00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:17.727
The ratio describes probabilities, not a promised batch.

33
00:02:19.107 --> 00:02:21.487
Random offspring can land unevenly.

34
00:02:22.787 --> 00:02:26.207
Larger samples usually settle closer to the predicted shares.

35
00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:32.130
Mendel's pea counts landed close, but not exactly on the predicted groups.

36
00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.030
That is what repeated chance events should look like.

37
00:02:37.033 --> 00:02:41.053
This pattern needs independent assortment and complete dominance.

38
00:02:42.513 --> 00:02:46.433
Linked genes, close together on one chromosome, can depart from it.

39
00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:49.893
Other allele relationships can change it too.

40
00:02:50.967 --> 00:02:53.627
One gene or two, the logic stays steady.

41
00:02:55.067 --> 00:02:59.147
Alleles separate, sex cells combine, and the square organizes the chances.

42
00:03:00.487 --> 00:03:05.747
Two independent genes simply give four sex-cell types and sixteen equal chances.
